in this episode of cloud performance  atlas I show how a new load balancer  protocol can give a big performance  boost while we walk the performance high  wire stay tuned to find out as we've  seen before Google clouds networking  suite offers reliable high performing  services for your cloud applications  things like TCP bbr network service  tiers load balancing see DNS DNS the  list goes on the gist is that GCPs  networking services give you a lot of  performance with very little heavy  lifting from your team and that trend  continues recently GCPs load balancer  added support for the quic protocol  quick or quick UDP internet connections  is a modern transport layer protocol  that provides congestion control similar  to TCP and security equivalent to SSL  not to mention improve performance most  of the magic of quic is concentrated on  reducing the number of round trips  required when establishing a new  connection including the handshake step  encryption step and initial data request  which makes it a great option in areas  where you see less than ideal  connectivity from your clients to test  it out we first created a simple HTTP  load balancer that serves a 10 megabyte  file from a GCS bucket then we went into  the console and enabled the quic  negotiation setting for the load  balancer which allows the balancer to  accept quick connections from clients  that support it without disturbing our  existing HTTPS users now it's worth  noting that much like TCP bbr the  benefits of quic are best seen in  situations where clients have poor  connectivity as such to get a true  performance test we simulate a bad  network connection using a TC command  for both our HTTP and quic clients now  on our basic tests quic saw a 1.3 X  improvement in overall performance  versus the standard HTTP on the load  balancer which is pretty nice for just  setting one flag if you're interested to  know more about the quic protocol on GCP  then check out the article linked in the  description below and don't forget to  subscribe to our youtube channel for  more great cloud performance Atlas  content because when it comes to  performance every millisecond counts
 

